basically when i talk about building  psychological safety on a team  what that means is leaders create an  environment which the people working in  the company the people on a team  feel safe enough to raise their hand and  say i made a mistake  or i don't understand what i'm doing or  i'm struggling at home and it's  affecting my work or  i'm scared or i need help without any  fear of humiliation or retribution  in fact they can say these things with  absolute confidence that their boss or  their co-workers will rush to support  them  that's what a trusting team feels like  we've all been on one before we also  all know what it feels like not to be on  a trusting team where we would never  admit mistakes for fear of humiliation  or getting in trouble  we're never going to say i don't  understand something um we're not going  to admit we're scared we're not going to  tell anybody that we're struggling at  home it's affecting our work and we're  definitely not asking for help  because these things make us vulnerable  or weak it might put us on a short list  for the next round of layoffs or worse  my boss it might hurt my career chances  i won't get promoted  because i've i've said i don't  understand something so we keep quiet  might work in the short term but over  the course of time a company where no  employee ever admits mistakes  or worse they hide their mistakes  eventually those cracks grow and grow  and grow and and  cause great damage to the organization  and its ability  uh to succeed so this is a leader's  responsibility  it's a leader's responsibility to create  an environment in which people feel  psychologically safe  and if somebody makes a mistake we can  help them fix it as alan mullally used  to talk about when he took over  ford murder company uh you are not the  problem you have a problem and if you  can tell me the problem  we can all rally together to help you  fix it  you
 

